[] + [] -> '' (Arrays are converted to strings. [].toString() is "", so "" + "" → "")
typeof null -> object
NaN === NaN -> false
0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3 -> false (Floating-point precision issue (0.30000000000000004))
[] == false -> true ([] → "" → 0, false → 0, so 0 == 0)
{} + {} -> "[object Object][object Object]" (because both objects are converted to strings: "[object Object]" + "[object Object]")
5 == "5" -> true
5 === "5" -> false
[] + {} -> "[object Object]"
null == undefined -> true
null === undefined -> false
typeof NaN -> number
{} + [] -> 0 (At the start of a statement, JavaScript interprets {} as an empty block rather than an object literal. The remaining expression becomes +[]. Unary plus converts the empty array to a number: [] → "" → 0, so the result is 0.)
Why?
JavaScript parses the first {} as an empty block statement, not an object literal.
So the engine sees:
{}
+[]

which is effectively: +[]
Now evaluate: +[]

Unary + converts the array to a number :- [] -> ""
then: Number("")
becomes: 0

Therefore:
+[] === 0

Result:
0

If you force both to be objects/arrays :- ({}) + []

Now the first {} is definitely an object literal.
Evaluation: ({}).toString() + [].toString()
becomes: "[object Object]" + ""

Result: "[object Object]"
